#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:5
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:5
msgid ""
"Vendored libraries **MUST** not be modified except as required to "
"successfully vendor them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:7
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:7
msgid ""
"Vendored libraries **MUST** be released copies of libraries available on "
"PyPI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:9
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:9
msgid ""
"Vendored libraries **MUST** be available under a license that allows them"
" to be integrated into ``pip``, which is released under the MIT license."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:11
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:11
msgid "Vendored libraries **MUST** be accompanied with LICENSE files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:12
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:12
msgid ""
"The versions of libraries vendored in pip **MUST** be reflected in "
"``pip/_vendor/vendor.txt``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:14
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:14
msgid ""
"Vendored libraries **MUST** function without any build steps such as "
"``2to3`` or compilation of C code, practically this limits to single "
"source 2.x/3.x and pure Python."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:17
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:17
msgid ""
"Any modifications made to libraries **MUST** be noted in "
"``pip/_vendor/README.rst`` and their corresponding patches **MUST** be "
"included ``tools/vendoring/patches``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:20
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:20
msgid ""
"Vendored libraries should have corresponding ``vendored()`` entries in "
"``pip/_vendor/__init__.py``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:24
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:24
msgid "Rationale"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:26
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:26
msgid ""
"Historically pip has not had any dependencies except for ``setuptools`` "
"itself, choosing instead to implement any functionality it needed to "
"prevent needing a dependency. However, starting with pip 1.5, we began to"
" replace code that was implemented inside of pip with reusable libraries "
"from PyPI. This brought the typical benefits of reusing libraries instead"
" of reinventing the wheel like higher quality and more battle tested "
"code, centralization of bug fixes (particularly security sensitive ones),"
" and better/more features for less work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:34
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:34
msgid ""
"However, there are several issues with having dependencies in the "
"traditional way (via ``install_requires``) for pip. These issues are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:37
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:37
msgid "**Fragility**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:38
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:38
msgid ""
"When pip depends on another library to function then if for whatever "
"reason that library either isn't installed or an incompatible version is "
"installed then pip ceases to function. This is of course true for all "
"Python applications, however for every application *except* for pip the "
"way you fix it is by re-running pip. Obviously, when pip can't run, you "
"can't use pip to fix pip, so you're left having to manually resolve "
"dependencies and installing them by hand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:46
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:46
msgid "**Making other libraries uninstallable**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:47
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:47
msgid ""
"One of pip's current dependencies is the ``requests`` library, for which "
"pip requires a fairly recent version to run.  If pip depended on "
"``requests`` in the traditional manner, then we'd either have to maintain"
" compatibility with every ``requests`` version that has ever existed (and"
" ever will), OR allow pip to render certain versions of ``requests`` "
"uninstallable. (The second issue, although technically true for any "
"Python application, is magnified by pip's ubiquity; pip is installed by "
"default in Python, in ``pyvenv``, and in ``virtualenv``.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:56
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:56
msgid "**Security**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:57
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:57
msgid ""
"This might seem puzzling at first glance, since vendoring has a tendency "
"to complicate updating dependencies for security updates, and that holds "
"true for pip. However, given the *other* reasons for avoiding "
"dependencies, the alternative is for pip to reinvent the wheel itself.  "
"This is what pip did historically. It forced pip to re-implement its own "
"HTTPS verification routines as a workaround for the Python standard "
"library's lack of SSL validation, which resulted in similar bugs in the "
"validation routine in ``requests`` and ``urllib3``, except that they had "
"to be discovered and fixed independently. Even though we're vendoring, "
"reusing libraries keeps pip more secure by relying on the great work of "
"our dependencies, *and* allowing for faster, easier security fixes by "
"simply pulling in newer versions of dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:70
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:70
msgid "**Bootstrapping**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:71
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:71
msgid ""
"Currently most popular methods of installing pip rely on pip's self-"
"contained nature to install pip itself. These tools work by bundling a "
"copy of pip, adding it to ``sys.path``, and then executing that copy of "
"pip. This is done instead of implementing a \"mini installer\" (to reduce"
" duplication); pip already knows how to install a Python package, and is "
"far more battle-tested than any \"mini installer\" could ever possibly "
"be."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:78
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:78
msgid ""
"Many downstream redistributors have policies against this kind of "
"bundling, and instead opt to patch the software they distribute to "
"debundle it and make it rely on the global versions of the software that "
"they already have packaged (which may have its own patches applied to "
"it). We (the pip team) would prefer it if pip was *not* debundled in this"
" manner due to the above reasons and instead we would prefer it if pip "
"would be left intact as it is now."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:85
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:85
msgid ""
"In the longer term, if someone has a *portable* solution to the above "
"problems, other than the bundling method we currently use, that doesn't "
"add additional problems that are unreasonable then we would be happy to "
"consider, and possibly switch to said method. This solution must function"
" correctly across all of the situation that we expect pip to be used and "
"not mandate some external mechanism such as OS packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:94
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:94
msgid "Modifications"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:96
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:96
msgid "``setuptools`` is completely stripped to only keep ``pkg_resources``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:97
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:97
msgid ""
"``pkg_resources`` has been modified to import its dependencies from "
"``pip._vendor``, and to use the vendored copy of ``platformdirs`` rather "
"than ``appdirs``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:100
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:100
msgid ""
"``packaging`` has been modified to import its dependencies from "
"``pip._vendor``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:102
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:102
msgid ""
"``CacheControl`` has been modified to import its dependencies from "
"``pip._vendor``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:104
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:104
msgid ""
"``requests`` has been modified to import its other dependencies from "
"``pip._vendor`` and to *not* load ``simplejson`` (all platforms) and "
"``pyopenssl`` (Windows)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:107
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:107
msgid ""
"``platformdirs`` has been modified to import its submodules from "
"``pip._vendor.platformdirs``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:110
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:110
msgid "Automatic Vendoring"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:112
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:112
msgid ""
"Vendoring is automated via the `vendoring "
"<https://pypi.org/project/vendoring/>`_ tool from the content of "
"``pip/_vendor/vendor.txt`` and the different patches in "
"``tools/vendoring/patches``. Launch it via ``vendoring sync . -v`` "
"(requires ``vendoring>=0.2.2``). Tool configuration is done via "
"``pyproject.toml``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:118
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:118
msgid ""
"To update the vendored library versions, we have a session defined in "
"``nox``. The command to upgrade everything is::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:123
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:123
msgid ""
"We do not upgrade ``setuptools``, because we only rely on "
"``pkg_resources``, and tracking every ``setuptools`` change is "
"unnecessary for our needs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:128
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:128
msgid "Managing Local Patches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:130
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:130
msgid ""
"The ``vendoring`` tool automatically applies our local patches, but "
"updating, the patches sometimes no longer apply cleanly. In that case, "
"the update will fail. To resolve this, take the following steps:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:134
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:134
msgid ""
"Revert any incomplete changes in the revendoring branch, to ensure you "
"have a clean starting point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:136
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:136
msgid ""
"Run the revendoring of the library with a problem again: ``nox -s "
"vendoring -- --upgrade <library_name>``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:138
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:138
msgid ""
"This will fail again, but you will have the original source in your "
"working directory. Review the existing patch against the source, and "
"modify the patch to reflect the new version of the source. If you ``git "
"add`` the changes the vendoring made, you can modify the source to "
"reflect the patch file and then generate a new patch with ``git diff``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:143
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:143
msgid ""
"Now, revert everything *except* the patch file changes. Leave the "
"modified patch file unstaged but saved in the working tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:145
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:145
msgid ""
"Re-run the vendoring. This time, it should pick up the changed patch file"
" and apply it cleanly. The patch file changes will be committed along "
"with the revendoring, so the new commit should be ready to test and "
"publish as a PR."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:151
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:151
msgid "Debundling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:153
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:153
msgid ""
"As mentioned in the rationale, we, the pip team, would prefer it if pip "
"was not debundled (other than optionally "
"``pip/_vendor/requests/cacert.pem``) and that pip was left intact. "
"However, if you insist on doing so, we have a semi-supported method (that"
" we don't test in our CI) and requires a bit of extra work on your end in"
" order to solve the problems described above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:159
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:159
msgid ""
"Delete everything in ``pip/_vendor/`` **except** for "
"``pip/_vendor/__init__.py`` and ``pip/_vendor/vendor.txt``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:161
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:161
msgid ""
"Generate wheels for each of pip's dependencies (and any of their "
"dependencies) using your patched copies of these libraries. These must be"
" placed somewhere on the filesystem that pip can access (``pip/_vendor`` "
"is the default assumption)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:165
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:165
msgid ""
"Modify ``pip/_vendor/__init__.py`` so that the ``DEBUNDLED`` variable is "
"``True``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:167
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:167
msgid ""
"Upon installation, the ``INSTALLER`` file in pip's own ``dist-info`` "
"directory should be set to something other than ``pip``, so that pip can "
"detect that it wasn't installed using itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:170
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:170
msgid ""
"*(optional)* If you've placed the wheels in a location other than "
"``pip/_vendor/``, then modify ``pip/_vendor/__init__.py`` so that the "
"``WHEEL_DIR`` variable points to the location you've placed them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:173
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:173
msgid ""
"*(optional)* Update the ``pip_self_version_check`` logic to use the "
"appropriate logic for determining the latest available version of pip and"
" prompt the user with the correct upgrade message."
msgstr ""

#: ../../venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:177
#: ../../venv/lib64/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/README.rst:177
msgid ""
"Note that partial debundling is **NOT** supported. You need to prepare "
"wheels for all dependencies for successful debundling."
msgstr ""

